 "result": "Built maze.py: reads the grid from stdin as bytes, builds a wall-padded flat boolean \"free\" array with numpy, and runs a frontier-based BFS where each level expands the whole frontier vectorially via the four index offsets (\u00b11, \u00b1Wp), masking against the free array and marking visited; it prints the shortest 4-neighbour S\u2192E distance or -1 (0 if S==E, -1 if S/E missing). Correctness verified against a plain deque BFS reference (ref.py) on the 40x40 t.txt and a random 300x300 maze (both agree: 604 on the 300x300 case), plus hand cases (S.#/.##/..E \u2192 4, S#E \u2192 -1, SE \u2192 1, S above E \u2192 1, walled-off \u2192 -1). Performance: ~0.19 s per 2000x2000 maze generated by gen.py with fixed seed (p=0.3 walls); bench.sh checks all correctness cases then times three 2000x2000 mazes (answers 4002, 3998, 4148), scoring 1000/seconds; registered assay baseline 1854 (\u22480.54 s for three mazes, noise 2.9%).",
